INT. ROLLER RINK - 2 A.M.

Disco ball dead. Floor lights low. Speakers hiss.

DANI, seventeen, laces her skates on the carpeted bench. Beside her, KOFI sits with his helmet in his lap. MEI leans on the rail, chewing a straw. RAFA, the baby of the group, sits cross-legged on the floor.

She stands. Wobbles. He catches her elbow.

DANI
Don't.

KOFI
I'm not.

He lets go. She rolls out onto the maple. Slow. Steady. Then faster.

MEI
(to the others)
We're not supposed to be here.

RAFA
The door was open.

MEI
Doors open when you break them.

Dani circles the rink and spins. Her arms go wide. For a moment she's lit by the red EXIT sign alone.

A light snaps on in the booth. A hand slams a switch. The music blares: a polka.

MR. LAZLO, seventies, in a bathrobe and a rink-guard jacket, lumbers out of the DJ booth. He holds a bat.

LAZLO
Who's closing this place?

DANI
Nobody, sir.

LAZLO
Then who's skating?

Kofi raises a hand. Mei raises a hand. Rafa, after a beat, raises both.

LAZLO (CONT'D)
(lowering the bat)
Eight dollars a head. Cash.
